+# Privacy and security operations
+
+## Data inventory and retention
+
+| Data | Purpose | Storage | Retention / deletion |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Up to 100 article IDs per reading list | Explicit reader bookmarks | Process memory, bounded to 5,000 sessions | Purged after 24 hours from the last save; earlier on eviction or restart. DELETE removes the current session immediately. |
+| Random session ID | Connect browser to saved articles | First-party, HttpOnly, Secure (production), SameSite=Strict cookie scoped to `/devtools-times/` | 24 hours from last save; removed when list becomes empty or via privacy controls. |
+| Search text | Filter public articles | Component memory in the browser | No server submission or persistent storage. |
+| Connection metadata | Deliver HTTP requests | Hosting infrastructure | Operator must document provider logging, retention, access restrictions, and deletion before deployment. |
+
+The application collects no credentials, profile fields, analytics events, or advertising identifiers. No sessions are created during ordinary browsing. Reading lists are ephemeral and local to one server process; they are not an account feature. Multiple replicas require session affinity, or a separately reviewed shared store with equivalent enforced TTL, encryption, and access controls.
+
+## Third-party service register
+
+| Service | Previous data exposure | Current status | Audit evidence |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Cloud Functions demo analytics / advertising | Page visits and browser metadata; executable third-party code | Removed | No independent report supplied; do not re-enable without review. |
+| Cloud Functions demo login / registration | Username and password | Disabled; forms collect no credentials | No independent report supplied; integration blocked pending review. |
+| Google Fonts | Reader IP address and connection metadata | Removed; system serif fallback | No active reader connection. |
+| Imgur / external demo image host | Reader IP address, possible credentials | Replaced with bundled photos; remote article image paths rejected | No active reader connection. |
+| Hosting / CDN | Request connection metadata; potentially logs | Necessary infrastructure; deployment review required | Provider, region, retention, contracts, and independent reports must be supplied by the operator. |
+| npm dependencies | Build and runtime code supply chain | Lockfile enforced; dependency advisory scan in CI | `pnpm audit` is an advisory scan, not independent certification. |
+
+No third-party audit or certification is claimed. Repository tests cannot commission an independent audit or verify a provider's internal practices.
+
+## Independent review process
+
+The operator owns vendor approval. Before adding or re-enabling a provider, obtain a current independent security assessment (for example a relevant SOC 2 Type II report or independent penetration test), review its scope and exceptions, and record the reviewer, review date, report reference, remediation owner, and next review date. Keep confidential reports in restricted storage, not this public repository.
+
+Review the actual data flow, purpose, minimum fields, subprocessors, processing region, retention, deletion/export guarantees, incident notification, and applicable data-processing agreement. Verify transport security, server-side credential handling, secret storage, least-privilege access, and log redaction. Authentication additionally needs real session management, abuse protection, and server-side validation; restoring the old demo forms is insufficient.
+
+Repeat vendor review annually and whenever data flows or subprocessors change. Arrange an independent application review before enabling accounts or other sensitive processing, and track remediation to completion. Current independent application audit status: **not performed**.
+
+## Automated checks
+
+- `pnpm test:privacy` builds the production server and runs HTTP tests for no automatic sessions, no remote resources, generated CSP hashes, cookie flags, isolation, deletion, exact-origin checks, body limits, and input minimization.
+- `pnpm audit:dependencies` checks production dependencies against published advisories and fails on any known severity. Review and remediate findings; do not bypass failures with a blanket exception.
+- CI runs these checks for changes and weekly. Advisory results are point-in-time evidence, not a security guarantee.
+
+## Deployment
+
+Serve production over HTTPS. Secure cookies are mandatory in production; only `astro dev` relaxes that flag for local development. Restrict trusted forwarded hosts to the actual deployment domains, strip client-supplied forwarding headers at the proxy, and redirect HTTP to HTTPS there. HSTS is emitted for HTTPS requests. Remove unused allowed hosts when changing deployments.
+
+Dynamic HTML and API responses receive no-store caching, CSP, framing protection, no-referrer, nosniff, and a restrictive permissions policy. Astro generates script/style hashes; middleware preserves them and adds frame-ancestors. ClientRouter was removed because Astro's CSP support does not support it. The Node adapter serves static files before Astro middleware: configure equivalent applicable security headers and HSTS on static responses at the CDN/proxy. Do not publicly cache session-specific HTML or reading-list API responses.
+
+Keep request bodies, cookies, authorization headers, and full query strings out of infrastructure logs; restrict operational access and define short retention. Disable memory/core dumps that could capture sessions. Before rollout, securely remove legacy filesystem session storage (`.astro/sessions` with the previous Node adapter defaults) and associated retained backups under the operator's deletion process. Previously issued provider credentials or provider-side records require separate deletion with that provider; this application cannot erase those historical records.
+
+Implementation references: [Astro configuration](https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/) and [OWASP secure headers guidance](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/HTTP_Headers_Cheat_Sheet.html). For the installed Astro version, server rendering emits CSP as an HTTP header; the local runtime implementation and HTTP tests verify that behavior.
